   "text": "BENBULBEN. Binn Ghulbain, 'Gulban's peak'. A great flat-topped limestone mountain, 526 metres, shaped by ice. It looks like a ship, a table and a wall all at once, and it's behind everything in Sligo.",
   "x": 34.0,
   "y": 20.0
  },
  {
   "text": "W. B. YEATS. Poet, playwright and Nobel laureate, 1865 to 1939. His mother's family were Sligo merchants and the county's in half his poems. He's buried at Drumcliffe, under Benbulben, as he asked.",
   "x": 30.0,
   "y": 36.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE LAKE ISLE OF INNISFREE. Yeats wrote: 'I will arise and go now, and go to Innisfree, and a small cabin build there.' The real isle is on Lough Gill. This pond is the gift shop's version.",
   "x": 40.0,
   "y": 42.0
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: W. B. Yeats, poet, spent boyhood summers in Sligo and lies buried at Drumcliffe.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "sligo_wb_yeats",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "W. B. Yeats"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "sligo_markievicz",
   "title": "Countess Markievicz",
   "text": "Constance Gore-Booth grew up at Lissadell House, north of Sligo, and married a Polish count to become Countess Markievicz. She fought in the 1916 Rising, and in 1918 became the first woman elected to the House of Commons. She never took her seat, on principle."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "sligo_michael_coleman",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Michael Coleman",
   "text": "Michael Coleman, a fiddler from Killavil in south Sligo, emigrated to New York and recorded there in the 1920s. His records crossed back over the Atlantic and taught a generation of Irish players the Sligo style: fast, flowing and full of twiddly bits."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "sligo_battle_of_books",
   "title": "The Battle of the Books",
   "text": "Tradition says Colmcille secretly copied a borrowed book of psalms, and the High King ruled against him: 'to every cow her calf, to every book its copy'. The quarrel led to a battle at Cúl Dreimhne, under Benbulben, in 561. Perhaps the first copyright case."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "sligo_barnacle_geese",
   "title": "Barnacle Geese",
   "text": "Every winter barnacle geese fly in from Greenland to graze the fields by Lissadell. Medieval writers believed they hatched from barnacles on driftwood, which made them a kind of fish, so some people ate them on fast days. The geese never agreed to this."
  }
 ],
